\title{Quotient Topology and Quotient Space}
\taxon{Definition}
\p{
    Let #{X} be a [topological space](def-000C) and let #{q:X\to Y} be a
    surjection. The \strong{quotient topology} on #{Y} is defined by
    ##{
        U\subseteq Y\text{ is open}
        \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
        q^{-1}(U)\text{ is open in }X.
    }
    With this topology, #{q} is called a \strong{quotient map}. It is the
    [finest topology](def-005X) on #{Y} for which #{q} is continuous.
}
\p{
    In particular, if #{\sim} is an [equivalence relation](def-000X) on #{X},
    the canonical surjection
    ##{
        q:X\longrightarrow X/{\sim},
        \qquad x\longmapsto[x]
    }
    equips the [quotient set](def-002U) #{X/{\sim}} with a topology. The
    resulting topological space is the \strong{quotient space} of #{X} by
    #{\sim}.
}
